Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.21,0:00:04.20,Cap,,0,0,0,,The night before Challenger launched,\Nits engineers said it was too cold to fly.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.24,0:00:06.21,Cap,,0,0,0,,They were overruled.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:06.91,0:00:09.17,Cap,,0,0,0,,This is the story of mission 51-L.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:16.31,0:00:19.23,Cap,,0,0,0,,Challenger was the second\Nspace shuttle to enter service,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:19.74,0:00:22.62,Cap,,0,0,0,,and it first flew in April 1983.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:23.86,0:00:27.30,Cap,,0,0,0,,On that first mission, the crew\Nlaunched a communications satellite,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:27.49,0:00:29.99,Cap,,0,0,0,,and made the first spacewalk\Nof the shuttle program.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:31.13,0:00:34.83,Cap,,0,0,0,,By January 1986 it had\Ncompleted nine missions,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:35.28,0:00:37.94,Cap,,0,0,0,,and mission 51-L was its tenth.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:39.04,0:00:42.36,Cap,,0,0,0,,NASA had promised that the shuttle\Nwould make spaceflight routine,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:42.55,0:00:47.17,Cap,,0,0,0,,and in 1985 it was planning\N24 flights a year by 1990.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:48.17,0:00:51.00,Cap,,0,0,0,,Mission 51-L would\Nalso carry a teacher.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:51.60,0:00:53.45,Cap,,0,0,0,,In July 1985,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:53.52,0:00:56.73,Cap,,0,0,0,,Vice President George Bush\Nannounced that Christa McAuliffe,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:56.82,0:00:58.18,Cap,,0,0,0,,of Concord, New Hampshire,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:58.41,0:01:01.28,Cap,,0,0,0,,had been chosen from more\Nthan 11,000 applicants.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:02.32,0:01:06.89,Cap,,0,0,0,,She trained with NASA, including\Nweightless flights on a KC-135 aircraft.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:08.17,0:01:11.74,Cap,,0,0,0,,She was to teach lessons from orbit,\Nto classrooms across the country.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:12.94,0:01:16.29,Cap,,0,0,0,,Another teacher, Barbara Morgan,\Ntrained beside her as her backup.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:17.43,0:01:20.31,Cap,,0,0,0,,The crew of seven also included\Ncommander Dick Scobee,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:20.72,0:01:23.53,Cap,,0,0,0,,pilot Michael Smith, Ellison Onizuka,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:23.84,0:01:27.42,Cap,,0,0,0,,Judith Resnik, Ronald\NMcNair, and Gregory Jarvis.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:28.80,0:01:31.60,Cap,,0,0,0,,The launch was planned\Nfor the 22nd of January.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:32.17,0:01:33.96,Cap,,0,0,0,,It was postponed three times,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:34.11,0:01:37.33,Cap,,0,0,0,,and then scrubbed on the 27th\Nbecause of high crosswinds.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:41.65,0:01:45.54,Cap,,0,0,0,,The forecast for that night called for\Ntemperatures in the low twenties,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:45.56,0:01:48.02,Cap,,0,0,0,,perhaps as low as 18\Ndegrees Fahrenheit.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:49.16,0:01:53.28,Cap,,0,0,0,,The solid rocket boosters were\Nbuilt by Morton Thiokol, in Utah.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:53.83,0:01:55.52,Cap,,0,0,0,,Each was assembled from segments,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:55.75,0:01:58.09,Cap,,0,0,0,,joined by seals made\Nof rubber O-rings.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:59.43,0:02:03.87,Cap,,0,0,0,,Thiokol engineer Roger Boisjoly had\Nalready warned about those joints.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:04.44,0:02:08.49,Cap,,0,0,0,,In a memo in July 1985, he wrote\Nthat the result of a failure would be,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:08.62,0:02:12.70,Cap,,0,0,0,,quote, a catastrophe of the\Nhighest order, loss of human life.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:13.90,0:02:18.74,Cap,,0,0,0,,In January 1985, a shuttle launched\Nwith its O-rings at 53 degrees,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:19.01,0:02:20.34,Cap,,0,0,0,,the coldest to that date.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:20.93,0:02:23.53,Cap,,0,0,0,,Afterward, erosion was\Nfound in both boosters.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:24.75,0:02:28.66,Cap,,0,0,0,,At 8:45 that evening, engineers\Nand managers at Thiokol,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:28.68,0:02:30.17,Cap,,0,0,0,,at Marshall Space Flight Center,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:30.30,0:02:32.84,Cap,,0,0,0,,and at Kennedy Space Center\Njoined a teleconference.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:33.88,0:02:37.70,Cap,,0,0,0,,Thiokol's engineers showed charts\Nof O-ring damage on earlier flights.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:38.37,0:02:42.01,Cap,,0,0,0,,Their recommendation was not to\Nlaunch below 53 degrees Fahrenheit.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:43.23,0:02:45.27,Cap,,0,0,0,,NASA's Marshall\Nmanagers pushed back.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:45.94,0:02:48.50,Cap,,0,0,0,,One of them, George Hardy,\Nsaid he was appalled.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:49.80,0:02:53.28,Cap,,0,0,0,,Larry Mulloy later testified that\Nhe may have said something like:
Dialogue: 0,0:02:53.73,0:02:55.85,Cap,,0,0,0,,when do you want me\Nto launch, next April?
Dialogue: 0,0:02:57.11,0:03:00.35,Cap,,0,0,0,,Thiokol asked for a few minutes\Noff the line to talk privately.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:00.78,0:03:02.92,Cap,,0,0,0,,The break lasted about 30 minutes.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:04.06,0:03:05.56,Cap,,0,0,0,,Boisjoly later testified
Dialogue: 0,0:03:05.58,0:03:09.32,Cap,,0,0,0,,that a senior manager told Thiokol's\Nvice president of engineering
Dialogue: 0,0:03:09.37,0:03:12.57,Cap,,0,0,0,,to take off his engineering hat\Nand put on his management hat.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:13.63,0:03:17.26,Cap,,0,0,0,,Boisjoly said that no engineer\Nspoke in favor of launching.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:18.24,0:03:21.19,Cap,,0,0,0,,When the teleconference\Nresumed, at about 11 p.m.,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:21.21,0:03:24.23,Cap,,0,0,0,,Thiokol's management\Nreversed its position,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:24.40,0:03:25.61,Cap,,0,0,0,,and recommended the launch.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:29.69,0:03:34.38,Cap,,0,0,0,,On the morning of the 28th, ice hung\Nfrom the launch tower at pad 39B.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:35.82,0:03:37.45,Cap,,0,0,0,,Rockwell, which built the orbiter,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:37.67,0:03:40.28,Cap,,0,0,0,,told NASA that the ice was\Nan unknown condition,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:40.43,0:03:42.65,Cap,,0,0,0,,and that it could not\Nassure it was safe to fly.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:43.85,0:03:46.75,Cap,,0,0,0,,The temperature at launch\Nwas 36 degrees Fahrenheit,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:47.12,0:03:49.68,Cap,,0,0,0,,15 degrees colder than\Nany previous launch.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:51.08,0:03:53.83,Cap,,0,0,0,,Challenger lifted off at\N11:38 in the morning.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:54.83,0:03:56.40,Cap,,0,0,0,,Less than one second later,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:56.54,0:03:58.72,Cap,,0,0,0,,cameras recorded a\Npuff of gray smoke
Dialogue: 0,0:03:58.74,0:04:01.38,Cap,,0,0,0,,at the aft joint of the right\Nsolid rocket booster.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:02.72,0:04:06.55,Cap,,0,0,0,,Investigators later concluded that\Nthe seal in that joint had failed.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:07.49,0:04:09.72,Cap,,0,0,0,,For almost a minute,\Nthe shuttle climbed.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:11.10,0:04:14.52,Cap,,0,0,0,,At about 59 seconds, a flame\Nappeared at the same joint.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:15.07,0:04:18.09,Cap,,0,0,0,,Within two seconds, it was\Nstriking the external tank.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:19.37,0:04:22.33,Cap,,0,0,0,,At 73 seconds,\NChallenger broke apart.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:23.06,0:04:24.80,Cap,,0,0,0,,All seven crew members were lost.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:25.86,0:04:26.58,Cap,,0,0,0,,That evening,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:26.60,0:04:29.44,Cap,,0,0,0,,President Reagan postponed\Nhis State of the Union address,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:29.63,0:04:31.71,Cap,,0,0,0,,and spoke to the nation\Nfrom the Oval Office.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:32.71,0:04:33.70,Cap,,0,0,0,,He spoke directly
Dialogue: 0,0:04:33.72,0:04:35.74,Cap,,0,0,0,,to the schoolchildren\Nwho had been watching.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:36.93,0:04:39.27,Cap,,0,0,0,,And I want to say something to the schoolchildren of America
Dialogue: 0,0:04:39.27,0:04:41.99,Cap,,0,0,0,,who were watching the live coverage of the shuttle's takeoff.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:43.03,0:04:47.13,Cap,,0,0,0,,I know it's hard to understand, but sometimes painful things like this happen.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:51.31,0:04:55.47,Cap,,0,0,0,,On the 31st of January, Reagan spoke\Nat a memorial service in Houston,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:55.76,0:04:57.50,Cap,,0,0,0,,and read the name of\Neach crew member.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:58.89,0:05:00.47,Cap,,0,0,0,,We remember Christa McAuliffe,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:01.57,0:05:03.91,Cap,,0,0,0,,who captured the imagination of the entire nation,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:05.01,0:05:08.87,Cap,,0,0,0,,inspiring us with her pluck, her restless spirit of discovery,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:10.09,0:05:12.37,Cap,,0,0,0,,On the 3rd of February,\Nhe named a commission,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:12.50,0:05:15.24,Cap,,0,0,0,,led by William Rogers, a\Nformer Secretary of State.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:16.30,0:05:19.18,Cap,,0,0,0,,Its members included Neil\NArmstrong, Sally Ride,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:19.41,0:05:22.11,Cap,,0,0,0,,and the Nobel Prize winning\Nphysicist Richard Feynman.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:23.27,0:05:25.69,Cap,,0,0,0,,At a public hearing on\Nthe 11th of February,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:26.06,0:05:28.50,Cap,,0,0,0,,Feynman reported what\Nhappened when he put a piece
Dialogue: 0,0:05:28.52,0:05:30.78,Cap,,0,0,0,,of the booster seal\Nrubber into ice water:
Dialogue: 0,0:05:31.21,0:05:32.48,Cap,,0,0,0,,it did not spring back.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:33.96,0:05:36.20,Cap,,0,0,0,,The Commission's\Nreport, delivered in June,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:36.41,0:05:38.69,Cap,,0,0,0,,said the cause was the\Nfailure of the pressure seal
Dialogue: 0,0:05:38.71,0:05:42.05,Cap,,0,0,0,,in the aft field joint of the\Nright solid rocket motor.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:43.19,0:05:46.73,Cap,,0,0,0,,The Commission found a serious\Nflaw in the decision making process.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:47.34,0:05:50.69,Cap,,0,0,0,,Had the engineers' doubts been\Nclearly stated and emphasized,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:50.84,0:05:53.88,Cap,,0,0,0,,it said, the launch might not\Nhave occurred when it did.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:55.16,0:05:56.90,Cap,,0,0,0,,At Marshall, a launch constraint
Dialogue: 0,0:05:56.92,0:05:59.80,Cap,,0,0,0,,on the booster joints had been\Nwaived six times in a row.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:00.39,0:06:02.60,Cap,,0,0,0,,The Commission found\Nthat the senior managers,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:02.77,0:06:05.73,Cap,,0,0,0,,Jesse Moore and Arnold\NAldrich, did not know.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:06.87,0:06:08.95,Cap,,0,0,0,,It called the joint a faulty design,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:09.32,0:06:13.28,Cap,,0,0,0,,unacceptably sensitive to a number\Nof factors, including temperature.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:14.72,0:06:15.71,Cap,,0,0,0,,It also found
Dialogue: 0,0:06:15.82,0:06:18.93,Cap,,0,0,0,,that Thiokol's management\Nhad reversed its position
Dialogue: 0,0:06:18.95,0:06:20.10,Cap,,0,0,0,,at Marshall's urging,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:20.33,0:06:22.40,Cap,,0,0,0,,contrary to its engineers' views,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:22.87,0:06:25.41,Cap,,0,0,0,,in order to accommodate\Na major customer.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:26.69,0:06:30.29,Cap,,0,0,0,,On the ice, the Commission found\Nthe decision to launch questionable,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:30.52,0:06:33.06,Cap,,0,0,0,,because NASA appeared to\Nbe requiring a contractor
Dialogue: 0,0:06:33.08,0:06:35.04,Cap,,0,0,0,,to prove it was not safe to launch,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:35.31,0:06:36.86,Cap,,0,0,0,,rather than proving that it was.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:38.16,0:06:41.38,Cap,,0,0,0,,Its first recommendation was that\Nthe faulty joint must be changed,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:41.87,0:06:45.06,Cap,,0,0,0,,and that no design option should\Nbe ruled out because of schedule,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:45.19,0:06:47.77,Cap,,0,0,0,,cost, or reliance on existing hardware.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:49.05,0:06:50.00,Cap,,0,0,0,,In his own appendix,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:50.39,0:06:52.46,Cap,,0,0,0,,Feynman wrote that\Nestimates of the chance
Dialogue: 0,0:06:52.48,0:06:55.33,Cap,,0,0,0,,of failure ranged from about 1 in 100,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:55.66,0:06:59.88,Cap,,0,0,0,,from working engineers, to 1 in\N100,000, from management.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:00.90,0:07:02.48,Cap,,0,0,0,,Feynman noted that odds of 1
Dialogue: 0,0:07:02.50,0:07:05.45,Cap,,0,0,0,,in 100,000 would mean\Nlaunching a shuttle every day
Dialogue: 0,0:07:05.47,0:07:07.24,Cap,,0,0,0,,for 300 years to lose one,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:07.73,0:07:09.68,Cap,,0,0,0,,and asked what lay\Nbehind management's,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:09.84,0:07:12.45,Cap,,0,0,0,,quote, fantastic faith\Nin the machinery.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:13.49,0:07:17.67,Cap,,0,0,0,,He ended with a line that became\Nfamous: for a successful technology,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:18.04,0:07:20.82,Cap,,0,0,0,,reality must take precedence\Nover public relations,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:20.97,0:07:22.56,Cap,,0,0,0,,for nature cannot be fooled.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:23.95,0:07:26.45,Cap,,0,0,0,,Was this an accident that did not have to happen?
Dialogue: 0,0:07:27.43,0:07:28.35,Cap,,0,0,0,,Yes, it was.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:28.59,0:07:33.35,Cap,,0,0,0,,It was an accident that had many, many warnings
Dialogue: 0,0:07:33.35,0:07:37.65,Cap,,0,0,0,,that there was something wrong and that it might sooner or later go off,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:38.33,0:07:40.37,Cap,,0,0,0,,and the warnings were disregarded.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:41.63,0:07:42.92,Cap,,0,0,0,,On the first anniversary,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:43.36,0:07:44.08,Cap,,0,0,0,,Reagan spoke
Dialogue: 0,0:07:44.10,0:07:47.84,Cap,,0,0,0,,to NASA employees about the lessons\NChrista McAuliffe had hoped to teach.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:49.11,0:07:52.09,Cap,,0,0,0,,Well, she never got to give those lessons in space science,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:52.77,0:07:56.41,Cap,,0,0,0,,but she and the others did teach America's children something nevertheless,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:57.11,0:07:59.31,Cap,,0,0,0,,something precious, something enduring,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:00.33,0:08:02.65,Cap,,0,0,0,,The shuttle was\Ngrounded for 32 months.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:03.24,0:08:07.24,Cap,,0,0,0,,Discovery flew again on\Nthe 29th of September 1988,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:07.33,0:08:08.80,Cap,,0,0,0,,with redesigned boosters.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:10.94,0:08:13.90,Cap,,0,0,0,,The engineers said not to\Nlaunch below 53 degrees,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:14.09,0:08:16.27,Cap,,0,0,0,,and that same night the\Nwarning was reversed.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:17.46,0:08:20.00,Cap,,0,0,0,,When the people closest\Nto the data say stop,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:20.51,0:08:22.49,Cap,,0,0,0,,who decides whether anyone listens?
